Q: How do I remove a style element from an inline style using regex? A: <p>Since your style is in a DOM element with explicit ID &quot;first_line_info&quot;, the simplest way to achieve your goal would be:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;script&gt;
  // Step 1: Get the element by its ID
  var element = document.getElementById(&quot;first_line_info&quot;);

  // Step 2: Remove the float style
  element.style.float = null;

&lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>If you want to make such code a function so that it can also apply to other elements, you can create a function, like so:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;script&gt;
  function removeFloatStyleById(elementId) {
    // Make sure the function has an element ID, which should always be a string
    if (elementId === undefined) {
      console.error(&quot;removeFloatStyleById requires an element ID argument.&quot;);
      return;
    }
    if (typeof elementId !== &quot;string&quot; || elementId.trim() === &quot;&quot;) {
      console.error(&quot;removeFloatStyleById requires a non-empty string ID.&quot;);
      return;
    }
    var element = document.getElementById(elementId);
    if (element) {
      element.style.float = null;
    } else { // Comment out this section if you don't care if the ID is unmatched
      console.warn(&quot;removeFloatStyleById - No element found with ID:&quot;, elementId);
    }
  }
&lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>... So that elsewhere you can use removeFloatStyleById(&quot;ID-of-the-elemenet&quot;) on the elements.<br />
As @vusan kindly noted and provided his answer previously, using DOM manipulation is a preferred way than Regex when CSS/style is involved.<br />
But according to your comment in question, it seems you wanted to remove the &quot;float&quot; property from not just multiple elements but all elements in the entire document. If so, here's a version for it (no function needed):</p>
<pre><code>&lt;script&gt;
  // Remove &quot;float&quot; style from all DOM elements.
  // Step 1: Select all elements in the document
  var elements = document.querySelectorAll(&quot;*&quot;);
  // Step 2: Loop through and check if they have inline float
  elements.forEach(function(element) {
    if (element.style &amp;&amp; element.style.float) {
      element.style.float = null;
    }
  });
&lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: Configure gradle projects in SonarQube A: <p>As Olaf points out: This question was also posted to <a href="https://web.liferay.com/community/forums/-/message_boards/message/104477676" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://web.liferay.com/community/forums/-/message_boards/message/104477676</a></p>

<p>You could configure all the subprojects as a different SonarQube project with the following in your <code>build.gradle</code>:</p>

<pre><code>subprojects{
  sonarqube {
      properties {
          property 'sonar.projectName', "${-&gt; project.name}"
      }
  }
}
</code></pre>

<p>You can also set the property <code>sonar.projectKey</code> or any other property from <a href="https://docs.sonarqube.org/display/SONAR/Analysis+Parameters" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://docs.sonarqube.org/display/SONAR/Analysis+Parameters</a></p>

<p>I took the idea of the lazily evaluated project name from: <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/27682676/how-can-i-make-gradle-extensions-lazily-evaluate-properties-that-are-set-dynamic">How can I make Gradle extensions lazily evaluate properties that are set dynamically by tasks?</a></p>

Q: How to perform Right Click on an object using SendKeys in UFT? A: <p>there is one shortcut for Keyboard right click Shift+F10.
If we pass this through send keys then we can able to do this.before doing this we have to either mouse over on that object or we have make that active by clicking on that object.</p>

<p>for Example</p>

<pre><code>Browser().page().webelement().click;
Browser().page().webelement().sendkeys "+F10";
</code></pre>

Q: Failed to create assembly: Error creating assembly archive null: A tar file cannot include itself A: <p>I have resolved this issue by adding &quot;maven-clean-plugin&quot;.</p>
<pre><code>&lt;plugin&gt;
            &lt;artifactId&gt;maven-clean-plugin&lt;/artifactId&gt;
            &lt;version&gt;3.1.0&lt;/version&gt;
            &lt;executions&gt;
                &lt;execution&gt;
                    &lt;id&gt;auto-clean&lt;/id&gt;
                    &lt;phase&gt;initialize&lt;/phase&gt;
                    &lt;goals&gt;
                        &lt;goal&gt;clean&lt;/goal&gt;
                    &lt;/goals&gt;
                &lt;/execution&gt;
            &lt;/executions&gt;
        &lt;/plugin&gt;
</code></pre>
